syntax:
Exceptions are thrown using the `throw` keyword, and caught using `try-catch` blocks.  Different `catch` blocks can handle different exception types.

```c++
//Basic Syntax
try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
  // Handle any exception inherited from std::exception
  std::cerr << "Exception caught: " << e.what() << '\n';
} catch (int errNum) {
  // Handle integer error codes
  std::cerr << "Error number: " << errNum << '\n';
} catch (...) {
  // Catch any other exception type (use cautiously)
  std::cerr << "An unknown exception occurred.\n";
}
```

**Note:** The `...` catch-all should be used sparingly and as the last resort, as it can mask unexpected errors.  It's generally better to catch specific exception types.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <exception>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <fstream>

void processFile(const std::string& filename) {
  std::ifstream inputFile(filename);
  if (!inputFile.is_open()) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Could not open file: " + filename);
  }
  // Process the file contents...
  inputFile.close();
}

int main() {
  try {
    processFile("my_file.txt");
  } catch (const std::runtime_error& error) {
    std::cerr << "Error: " << error.what() << std::endl;
    return 1; // Indicate failure
  } catch (const std::exception& e) {
    std::cerr << "An unexpected error occurred: " << e.what() << std::endl;
    return 1;
  }
  return 0; // Indicate success
}
